// REJECTS: Unexpected token 'x'. Valid alternatives: ['&&', '||', '==', '!=', '->'
//
// `let` has no declaration-only form. There is no `let x;` awaiting assignment, and the
// error proves why: `let` is not a statement keyword the parser dispatches on. It is
// listed in the identifier rule, so `let` was read as an ordinary expression and then `x`
// had nowhere to go -- the alternatives offered are binary operators, not '='.
//
// A rewrite that special-cases `let` as a statement keyword (the natural implementation)
// makes `let x;` a plausible declaration, and also quietly loses the ability to name a
// property `let`, which m3-expressions.pure and the identifier rule both permit.
###Pure
function fixture::neg::emptyLet(): Integer[1]
{
   let x;
   1;
}
